---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How it works — MeshWhisper"
  description="The two-layer architecture, namespace isolation, shared relay mesh, and privacy properties of MeshWhisper."
>
  <Nav current="How it works" />

  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-20">

    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-4">How it works</h1>
    <p class="text-slate-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-16">
      The core insight is simple. The consequences are significant.
    </p>

    <!-- Core insight -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The core insight</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        A relay does not need to read a message to forward it. If messages are encrypted before they leave
        the sending device, a relay is just a pipe. It receives an opaque blob, identifies the intended
        recipient by a hash, and forwards it. The message arrives at the recipient's device and is decrypted
        there, locally, with a key the relay never held.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The consequence is that the relay infrastructure for one application is identical — in every
        technical respect — to the relay infrastructure for every other application. A relay forwarding
        encrypted packets for a fitness app is indistinguishable from one forwarding for a marketplace.
        The relay cannot tell them apart.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700 rounded-xl px-6 py-4 font-mono text-brand-300 text-sm my-6">
        Relay promiscuously. Connect selectively.
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        The relay mesh is shared across all participating applications. At the session layer, a device
        only decrypts and surfaces messages belonging to its own namespace. Everything else passes through
        silently and ephemerally — never written to disk.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Namespace isolation -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Namespace isolation</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Applications sharing relay infrastructure are isolated cryptographically, not by access control.
        Each application is identified by a namespace derived from the developer's public key and the
        application bundle identifier. Destination hashes — the routing identifiers on every packet — are
        computed as:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 font-mono text-sm text-brand-300 my-6">
        BLAKE3(namespace_id || peer_key || epoch_hour)
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        A relay node cannot determine which application a packet belongs to, cannot inject into any namespace
        without knowing the identifier, and cannot correlate traffic across namespaces even under active
        observation. The isolation is structural. Hashes rotate every hour, limiting traffic-analysis windows.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Node layer -->
    <section id="node-layer" class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The node layer</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The node layer is the backbone. When a developer integrates the SDK, they deploy a MeshWhisper Node —
        a single lightweight binary that provides four functions in one container:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 mb-6">
        {[
          ['Packet relay and store-and-forward', 'Routes encrypted packets to connected clients. Queues blobs for offline recipients with a configurable TTL (default 72 hours).'],
          ['Push notification forwarding', 'When a message arrives for an offline device, fires a silent content-free wake signal via APNs, FCM, or Web Push. The wake signal contains no message content.'],
          ['Encrypted media storage', 'Accepts and serves opaque encrypted blobs for media messages. Never holds a decryption key. TTL-based expiry.'],
          ['Prekey directory', 'Stores public pre-key bundles for X3DH first contact. Allows strangers to initiate a session without prior communication.'],
        ].map(([title, body]) => (
          <li class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-lg p-4">
            <div class="text-white font-medium text-sm mb-1">{title}</div>
            <div class="text-slate-400 text-sm">{body}</div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        The node handles what phones cannot: it is always on, always connectable, and holds messages for
        offline recipients. It is a post box, not a postman. It never reads what it carries.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Device / mesh layer -->
    <section id="mesh" class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The device layer</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Every device running any SDK-embedded application is simultaneously a potential relay for every
        other such device. The mesh emerges from participation — it is not deployed, it accumulates.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        When two devices are nearby they relay for each other directly using platform-native P2P
        (Apple Multipeer Connectivity on iOS, Google Nearby Connections on Android).
        When further apart but on the same network, they relay over the local subnet.
        Over the internet, connectable devices — laptops on broadband, desktops — act as peers.
        Phones on cellular maintain outbound connections to their deployed node.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-yellow-800/40 rounded-xl p-5 text-sm text-slate-400 mb-4">
        <span class="text-yellow-500 font-medium">Roadmap.</span> The device mesh layer is specified and
        partially scaffolded in the SDK. The node layer is fully implemented and live.
        Stage 1 is the node layer. The mesh emerges in Stage 3 as SDK adoption grows.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Privacy -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Privacy that strengthens with adoption</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        This is the inverse of centralised messaging, where scale concentrates metadata and creates an
        increasingly valuable surveillance target. In MeshWhisper, scale distributes metadata.
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-4 mb-6">
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="text-slate-600 font-mono text-xs w-24 shrink-0 pt-1">Low density</div>
          <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Messages route Alice → Alice's node → Bob's node → Bob. Each node sees one end of the path.
            Privacy is roughly equivalent to a messaging service provider's view.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="text-slate-600 font-mono text-xs w-24 shrink-0 pt-1">High density</div>
          <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Messages route through multiple intermediate nodes. No single relay can correlate sender and
            recipient. Privacy approaches a backbone ISP's view: aware that traffic flows, unable to say
            meaningfully between whom.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div class="text-slate-600 font-mono text-xs w-24 shrink-0 pt-1">Always</div>
          <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Every active device generates a continuous stream of random encrypted packets — chaff — at a
            constant rate regardless of real message activity. A node operator cannot distinguish real
            messages from background noise.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        MeshWhisper is not anonymous communication in the strong sense — it is not Tor. Users requiring
        strong anonymity should use a VPN alongside the SDK. The protocol's privacy properties are real,
        bounded, and transparent about what they don't provide.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Bootstrap -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The bootstrap problem, honestly</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The node layer has value from the first integration. A developer who deploys a MeshWhisper Node
        gets working E2EE relay infrastructure immediately. That is a real outcome.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The distinctive value is in the mesh layer, and the mesh layer requires adoption that does not exist
        on day one. Multi-node privacy routing, ambient relay capacity, and privacy-improves-with-scale are
        all functions of how many nodes and devices are participating.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        This is the BitTorrent dynamic honestly applied. BitTorrent had no peers for the first torrent.
        MeshWhisper has no mesh for the first integration. What the architecture ensures is that each stage
        of adoption delivers real, usable value. The ceiling scales with adoption; the floor is set by the
        node layer on day one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="border-t border-slate-800 pt-8 flex gap-6 text-sm">
      <a href="/security" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300 transition-colors">Security and cryptography →</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/antonmannering/meshwhisper/blob/main/docs/whitepaper.md" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-slate-300 transition-colors">Read the whitepaper →</a>
    </div>

  </main>

  <footer class="border-t border-slate-800 mt-12">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-8 text-sm text-slate-600 flex justify-between">
      <span>MeshWhisper — MIT licensed</span>
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-slate-400 transition-colors">← Home</a>
    </div>
  </footer>

</Layout>
